WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.150
PQC for TLS and web services prepares web communication for a future

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.779
where some current public-key algorithms may no longer provide enough protection.

00:00:09.779 --> 00:00:13.566
TLS protects browsers, APIs, service-to-service calls, mobile backends, gateways,

00:00:13.566 --> 00:00:15.670
SaaS platforms, and internal applications.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:19.457
The migration challenge is not simply choosing an algorithm.

00:00:19.457 --> 00:00:23.666
It is understanding how handshake roles, certificates, clients, libraries, proxies,

00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:25.349
and operations fit together.

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.136
The most important technical distinction is between cryptographic roles.

00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:33.344
Key establishment helps create shared key material for the session.

00:00:33.344 --> 00:00:36.711
Digital signatures help authenticate identities and verify integrity.

00:00:36.711 --> 00:00:40.498
Symmetric encryption protects the application data after the handshake.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:44.706
Post-quantum KEMs, such as ML-KEM conceptually, relate to key establishment.

00:00:44.706 --> 00:00:48.493
Post-quantum signature algorithms, such as ML-DSA and SLH-DSA conceptually,

00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:51.439
relate to authentication and signing use cases.

00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:57.331
Hybrid key exchange can help during the transition, but it should be standards-based, tested,

00:00:57.331 --> 00:00:58.593
monitored, and governed.

00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:03.643
It does not remove the need for compatibility work or downgrade awareness.

00:01:03.643 --> 00:01:08.272
Certificate and PKI migration is its own ecosystem challenge involving browsers,

00:01:08.272 --> 00:01:11.638
operating systems, certificate authorities, trust stores, libraries, devices,

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:12.901
and enterprise products.

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:17.950
Web trust changes only safely when the whole validation path is ready.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.896
Strong readiness starts with inventory and prioritization.

00:01:20.896 --> 00:01:26.367
Find TLS endpoints, classify data, identify owners, map clients, track libraries and vendors,

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:31.837
test in labs, deploy in stages, monitor production signals, and prepare rollback plans.

00:01:31.837 --> 00:01:34.362
The goal is not panic-driven replacement.

00:01:34.362 --> 00:01:38.570
The goal is practical crypto agility for critical web services,

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:43.620
so organizations can adapt deliberately as standards, libraries, and vendor support mature.
